pidfd-attribution
=================

This research sensor records descriptor opener, pidfd_getfd acquirer, and
writer identities. Optional CPython frames describe the source observed at
those operations. Captured Python metadata is not independently attested.

Backends
--------
endpoint-service/  Continuous, module-free systemd collector. Start here for
                   endpoints that prohibit third-party kernel modules.
module-free/       Bounded fixture validation using upstream BPF helpers.
repository root    Bounded fixture validation with an out-of-tree helper module.
                   Requires module loading and lockdown=none.

Each backend keeps native sources in core/ and Python drivers in python/.
Canonical shared implementations live in shared/core/ and shared/python/;
backend symlinks are required. Clone the whole repository with symlinks intact.

Requirements
------------
Ubuntu 22.04 with HWE Linux 6.8+, Ubuntu 24.04, or Ubuntu 26.04; x86_64 or
arm64. CPython 3.10-3.14 release builds with a supported, on-target-generated
layout. Preflight and real BPF load/attachment decide support, not release names.
Recent live endpoint validation used Ubuntu 24.04/x86_64/Linux 6.8/Python 3.12.
Build-only evidence does not establish attachment support on another target.

The current attachment configuration requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Confidentiality
lockdown is unsupported. Full/RT/lazy preemption is refused: the current collector
uses per-CPU scratch. Dynamic kernels must expose their selected mode through
/sys/kernel/debug/sched/preempt. The selected mode must remain none or voluntary
while the sensor runs; a boot configuration fixed to that mode is preferable.
Integrity lockdown blocked this debugfs read on the tested Ubuntu 24.04 kernel.
Such hosts are refused even by the module-free backend; boot arguments alone
do not prove the running preemption mode. Firmware Secure Boot was not tested.

Endpoint quick start
--------------------
cd endpoint-service
sudo ./install-ubuntu.sh
sudo ./run.sh doctor
sudo ./run.sh build
sudo ./run.sh install --config config.example.json
sudo systemctl enable --now iosec-endpoint
sudo ./run.sh status --config /etc/iosec-endpoint.json
sudo ./run.sh events --config /etc/iosec-endpoint.json --follow --writes-only

The installed CLI is /opt/iosec-endpoint/python/service.py. Its doctor command
checks runtime admission without requiring the build toolchain. Rebuild and
reinstall after kernel changes or a change of the monitored Python binary.
Installation preserves existing policy and refuses to replace an existing /opt
installation. Stop the service and move the old installation before replacing it.

Configuration and coverage
--------------------------
config.example.json lists supported options. capture_python=false collects
identity records only. state_entries is bounded to 128-2048, with an additional
256 MiB conservative map-memory admission budget inside the collector. The
unit allows 512 MiB total; retained event data and workloads still need sizing.

Write attribution currently covers write(2), not writev, pwrite, sendfile,
copy_file_range, io_uring, or mmap writes. path_prefix matches the raw opening
name, not a canonical path: relative names, symlinks, and alternate names can
bypass it. cgroup_id selects one exact cgroup v2 ID, not descendants. Global
uprobes impose breakpoint overhead on every process mapping the selected
Python executable, even when policy excludes it. Adaptive degradation keeps
lifecycle bookkeeping attached while skipping Python stack decoding.

Descriptor-state loss and ring drops mark history gaps. Optional source-cache
pressure is reported separately. Missing interpreter callbacks remain a source
attribution limitation. Recovery preserves continuously observed live bindings;
source snapshots from an earlier capture epoch are rejected. Cleanup only scans
for indexed objects, including under index pressure. Kernels without an attachable
close_files hook retain the indexed kmem_cache_free hook and its host-wide cost.

Checks and evidence
-------------------
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_*.py'
python3 endpoint-service/tests/test_service.py
python3 -O -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_*.py'
python3 tools/check_style.py  (requires Black 24.2.0 and clang-format 18.1.3)

Hosted CI runs offline checks and the real collector storage tests on every
push and pull request. The storage job compiles without BPF attachment; it does
not establish kernel compatibility. The opt-in VM
workflows perform kernel validation. sudo ./run.sh validate in a fixture backend
runs its owned controls. Validation JSONs under validation/ record both passing
and rejected runs; historical reports pin historical source hashes. QEMU CPU
screens are not fleet benchmarks. This remains a research sensor, not a general
endpoint EDR or a production qualification claim.

Return-handler ordering is calibrated with an owned nested native call during
preparation. The measured bias is pinned alongside kernel BTF, not inferred
from the release number. Kernel 7.0 builds selecting lazy/full preemption remain
outside the current scratch backend's deployment support.

The endpoint skips and counts malformed payloads only inside valid ring-record
boundaries; these losses mark history gaps. Invalid ring boundaries stop the
collector. Partial event writes are rolled back before a disk/quota-full retry.
During storage blockage the service retains pending ring bytes, services its
watchdog, and retries each second. Ring overflow remains an explicit loss. A full
filesystem may also prevent fresh health files; status then becomes stale.
Automatic restarts continue at ten-second intervals until the fault is resolved.
Published evidence normalizes local host identities while preserving original
artifact hashes.

Fixture failure controls
------------------------
After building module-free/, sudo python3 tests/fixture_failures_guest.py
from the repository root checks loader error exits 5, 6 and 7 in an owned guest.
Each control verifies that its child is reaped and BPF program IDs are restored.
Mandatory Python driver and guest-control checks remain active with python -O.
The offline string-helper test exercises bounds and zero tails with native read
and loop stubs; live BPF string controls provide separate kernel evidence.
